Defer fires at end of a unit fn with implicit return
====================================================

When a `-> unit` function falls off the end of its body
(no explicit `return`), every `defer stmt` queued during the
function still runs in LIFO order before the function returns
to its caller. This pins that the defer-cleanup epilogue is
wired into the implicit-exit path, not only the explicit-
`return` path.

Sibling file `defer_lifo.lsysl` pins LIFO ordering with three
defers; this file focuses specifically on the implicit-exit
surface: zero defers, one defer, and one defer reached via a
conditional path that *doesn't* `return`.

Defers fire LIFO on the same `*Trace` helper used elsewhere in
this directory: bodies write into a string field through a
pointer, the test asserts the assembled order after the
helper returns. The helper itself is `-> unit` to dodge the
TRISC defer-with-value-return bug (see
feedback_sysl_trisc_defer_nonunit_return.md).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct DnrTrace
        s: string

Single defer, no explicit return
--------------------------------

The body has one `defer` and no statements after it. The fn
falls off the end implicitly. The defer must fire before
control returns to the caller.

    dnr_one_defer(t: *DnrTrace) -> unit
        defer t.s = t.s + "A"

    #test
    test_one_defer_implicit_exit() -> unit
        var t = DnrTrace("")
        dnr_one_defer(&t)
        assert(t.s == "A", s"expected 'A', got '${t.s}'")

Multiple statements after defer
-------------------------------

The defer is queued early; the body then does more work that
also mutates the trace; the fn falls off the end. The
non-deferred statements run in order, then the deferred body
runs last.

    dnr_defer_then_work(t: *DnrTrace) -> unit
        defer t.s = t.s + "D"
        t.s = t.s + "1"
        t.s = t.s + "2"

    #test
    test_defer_runs_after_body() -> unit
        var t = DnrTrace("")
        dnr_defer_then_work(&t)
        assert(t.s == "12D", s"expected '12D' (body then defer), got '${t.s}'")

Defer queued inside a taken if-branch, no return
------------------------------------------------

The defer is reached only inside a conditional that *is* taken
here. Defer scope is the **enclosing function**, not the
enclosing block — so a defer queued inside an `if` body still
fires at function exit, after all post-branch statements.

(The companion case — branch *not* taken, so the defer was
never dynamically queued — is currently a 6-of-7 codegen bug:
every codegen backend fires the defer anyway, only the
interpreter honours dynamic queuing. See
feedback_sysl_codegen_defer_in_skipped_branch.md for repro and
suspect codegen sites. The branch-skipped test was authored
but withheld pending a codegen fix.)

    dnr_defer_in_branch(t: *DnrTrace) -> unit
        if true
            defer t.s = t.s + "B"
            t.s = t.s + "x"
        t.s = t.s + "y"

    #test
    test_defer_in_taken_branch_fires_at_fn_exit() -> unit
        var t = DnrTrace("")
        dnr_defer_in_branch(&t)
        // Branch writes "x", post-branch writes "y", then defer "B" fires
        // at function exit (defer scope = function, not block).
        assert(t.s == "xyB", s"expected 'xyB', got '${t.s}'")

Zero defers — sanity baseline
-----------------------------

A `-> unit` fn with no defers at all and an implicit exit
should run normally and observably return; the trace just
contains whatever the body wrote.

    dnr_no_defers(t: *DnrTrace) -> unit
        t.s = t.s + "plain"

    #test
    test_no_defers_implicit_exit() -> unit
        var t = DnrTrace("")
        dnr_no_defers(&t)
        assert(t.s == "plain", s"expected 'plain', got '${t.s}'")
